           "A tiny mottled brown insect on a young citrus leaf; trees with yellowing veins and blotchy leaves.",
           "Farmers in Chaozhou, in southern China, were talking about a 'yellow dragon' disease of citrus by the 1870s, for the yellowing veins it causes; that is the meaning of huanglongbing. It was formally described in 1919. The cause is a *Liberibacter* bacterium, carried from tree to tree by the Asian citrus psyllid, a bug 2 to 3 mm long, or in Africa by a related psyllid. Leaves mottle and fall, roots decay, fruit drops early, and the tree dies. It has hit China, India, Brazil, Mexico and Florida. Growers fight the insect, replant, and hope for resistant trees; in July 2026 a trade report said the USDA had authorised the first HLB-resistant orange variety for planting.",
